# ALIS Component License Policy

This file is the sole operative source of truth for licenses applied by ALIS.
It assigns unmodified standard licenses to rights-cleared first-party material
distributed by ALIS under grants from its respective copyright holders. It
does not relicense Unreal Engine, Marketplace content, third-party
dependencies, or upstream datasets.

Only rights-cleared first-party material receives an ALIS assignment.
Contribution intake follows [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Tagged source
and Product releases follow
[docs/legal/release_compliance.md](docs/legal/release_compliance.md).

## 1. Precedence

Determine a file or artifact's terms in this order:

1. Its explicit third-party notice or release manifest.
2. The component assignment below, inherited from the owning native package or
   architectural boundary and classified by how it is integrated and
   distributed.
3. If neither applies, no public license is granted. Stop distribution until the
   owner and terms are recorded.

ALIS defaults apply only to rights-cleared first-party portions. Accurate
copyright, attribution, license, and proprietary notices remain operative and
must be retained. Third-party and contributor-specific obligations may
accumulate with the ALIS default. If the obligations cannot coexist, isolate,
rewrite, or exclude the material.

This policy applies to repository revisions that contain it. Previously
distributed revisions and releases retain the grants distributed with those
versions; a later policy does not revoke those grants.

First-party source files do not repeat component license identifiers. Preserve
every accurate ownership or upstream notice. The neutral license pointer may
follow those notices:

```text
License terms: see repository root LICENSE.
```

Package manifests may carry a standard license identifier when their ecosystem
requires one. That metadata must match this file.

A file's assignment is fixed by its owning component and declared distribution
boundary in the repository revision being distributed. Later deployment does
not silently relicense the file. A file that would cross or straddle a boundary
must be split or reclassified here before distribution.

## 2. Rights-Cleared First-Party Software

| Class | Component | License | Boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ue-in-process` | Code loaded, linked, imported, or executed inside Unreal | MPL-2.0 | Includes first-party C++, Build.cs, targets, UE Python, editor automation, Blueprint logic, and UE-facing configuration |
| `separate-process` | Separate ALIS executables, services, compilers, CLIs, and automation | AGPL-3.0-only | Must remain a separate process communicating through files or a documented protocol |
| `interoperability` | Protocol definitions, generated bindings, and minimal interoperability SDKs explicitly marked for this boundary | Apache-2.0 | Must not contain gameplay, authority, persistence, compiler, or runtime implementation logic |
| `documentation` | Documentation | MPL-2.0 | Includes project documentation and website source unless a file says otherwise |

The complete MPL and AGPL texts are:

- [MPL-2.0](LICENSES/MPL-2.0.txt)
- [AGPL-3.0-only](LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-only.txt)

Apache-2.0 becomes applicable only when an interoperability package is
explicitly marked `Apache-2.0`. Its canonical text is published by the
[Apache Software Foundation](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt).

## 3. Rights-Cleared First-Party Assets and Data

| Material | Terms |
|---|---|
| Public original art, audio, models, materials, animations, and other non-code assets explicitly marked for public reuse | CC BY 4.0 |
| Public Blueprint or other code-bearing asset source | MPL-2.0 |
| Synthetic non-code fixtures explicitly marked for unrestricted test reuse | CC0 1.0 |
| Private game content explicitly classified for packaged-product use, including hero art, story, music, levels, and world identity | No public reuse license; packaged use requires the Product terms |
| Unclassified or unmarked material | Distribution blocked until ownership, component, and terms are recorded |
| ALIS-authored database explicitly released for reuse | The license named in its release manifest |
| Generated terrain, roads, buildings, cells, or databases | The result calculated from the complete provenance graph |
| Third-party content or data | Original provider terms only |

CC BY 4.0 becomes applicable only through an explicit asset sidecar or release
manifest. It is not a blanket license for `Content/**` or every `.uasset`.
The canonical terms are published by
[Creative Commons](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode.en).

CC0 1.0 becomes applicable only through an explicit fixture sidecar or release
manifest. Its canonical terms are published by
[Creative Commons](https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.en).

ODbL, CDLA, CC0, provider-specific, or other data terms apply only where the
source ledger and release manifest identify them. ALIS cannot replace upstream
database or content terms with an ALIS software license.

## 4. Unreal and Third-Party Exclusions

No ALIS license applies to:

- Unreal Engine code, tools, Examples, Starter Content, or other Epic-owned
  technology;
- Fab, Marketplace, Megascans, MetaHuman, or separately licensed source
  content;
- any third-party portion explicitly governed by a different license or
  proprietary notice.

Those materials remain governed by their original agreements and must carry
their nearest component-local notice or provenance record. Free download,
installation with Unreal, or inclusion in the private working repository does
not grant public redistribution or relicensing rights.

## 5. Contributions

ALIS uses inbound-equals-outbound licensing:

- a contribution uses the license assigned to its component;
- contributors retain copyright;
- maintainers receive no private relicensing grant;
- submitting a contribution represents that the contributor has authority to
  provide it under the assigned component license; and
- copied code, generated vendor code, assets, data, and dependencies require
  their exact upstream provenance and compatible terms.

See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for the contribution process.

## 6. Trademarks

Software and content licenses do not grant rights to the ALIS name, logo, or
official identity. See [TRADEMARKS.md](TRADEMARKS.md).

## 7. Compliance Sources

- Compatibility rationale and dependency gates:
  [docs/legal/component_license_policy.md](docs/legal/component_license_policy.md)
- Boundary inheritance and generated effective-component reports:
  [docs/legal/component_license_policy.md#4-component-boundary-inheritance](docs/legal/component_license_policy.md#4-component-boundary-inheritance)
- World data, generated artifacts, and `.uasset` boundaries:
  [docs/legal/world_data_and_asset_policy.md](docs/legal/world_data_and_asset_policy.md)
- Packaged Product terms, notices, credits, and Unreal release checks:
  [docs/legal/release_compliance.md](docs/legal/release_compliance.md)

When a summary conflicts with a referenced standard license, the standard
license text controls. When a third-party notice conflicts with an ALIS
component default, the third-party notice controls and the material must be
isolated.

Every public source tree must include this file, applicable standard texts,
local notices, and provenance records. A tagged source release also includes
the generated effective-component manifest defined by the release gate.
